.TH CAMBER 1
.SH NAME
camber \- a side-view racer, and a course designer for it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/camber
[
.B -e
.I track
] [
.B -t
|
.B -s
[
.B -n
.I frames
] ] [
.B -q
] [
.I track
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Camber
is a racer in the shape that genre took on the early consoles.
Four lanes run left to right and the machine moves between them;
ramps throw it into the air, where it no longer steers but rotates,
and the attitude it is in when the wheels come back decides whether the
landing is clean or whether it goes down;
mud holds the speed to a crawl and strips give it back;
rivals occupy lanes and touching one is a fall.
A gauge reads the engine temperature: the hot throttle is faster and
fills the gauge, and a full gauge seizes the engine for a hundred frames.
The choice the whole game is made of is when
.I not
to use it.
.PP
.I Track
is the course to race, and defaults to
.BR /lib/tiles/ridge.trk .
.SS The course is a file
Every other game in
.B /dis/games
compiles its art and its levels into the program.
This one holds none.
.B /lib/tiles/camber.art
declares the tile sheets, the font and every palette, and is itself a
script in
.IR tiles (2)'s
command language rather than a format this program invented:
.I camber
runs it line by line at start-up.
The tiles it names are
.B /lib/tiles/camber.chr
and
.BR /lib/tiles/camber16.chr .
The course is a text file that
.B -e
writes,
that a shell script can generate,
and that an editor can edit.
There is not one tile, not one colour and not one feature of any course
in
.BR camber.b .
.PP
A track file is a header of
.I "KEY VALUE"
lines, then a line holding
.BR % ,
then one feature a line:
.IP
.EX
X LANE FEATURE [SPEED]
.EE
.PP
.I X
is in tiles of eight logical pixels from the start line,
.I LANE
counts inward from nought, and
.I FEATURE
is one of
.BR ramp ,
.BR bump ,
.BR mud ,
.B strip
or
.BR rider .
.I SPEED
is a rider's pace in hundredths of a pixel a frame and is ignored for
anything else.
Lines beginning with
.B ;
are comments.
The header settings are
.BR title ,
.B length
in tiles, and
.BR lanes ,
which may be one to four.
A course must be at least thirty-six tiles long, because the camera
assumes there is more course than screen.
A file that is wrong is refused with the line it went wrong on.
.SS Racing
Arrow keys or
.B wasd
move between lanes.
.BR x ,
.B .
or space is the throttle;
.B z
or
.B ,
is the hot one.
.B a
or left brakes.
In the air, left and right rotate the machine: left brings the nose up,
right brings it down, and the wheels must be somewhere near level when
they arrive.
Enter restarts a finished race.
.B q
or Escape quits.
The best time is kept by
.IR scorestore (2).
.SS Designing
.B -e
.I track
opens a course for editing instead of racing.
The name is not optional, because the designer writes back the file it
opened and the default is the course that ships with the game.
A name that does not exist yet starts an empty course of two hundred
tiles and four lanes, which is how a new one begins.
Arrow keys move the cursor, which shows the size of the feature about to
be laid;
.B [
and
.B ]
choose which feature that is;
space or Enter lays it;
.B d
removes whatever is under the cursor;
and
.B w
writes the file back.
Laying a feature clears everything it covers, so features never overlap.
.B q
or Escape quits without writing, so
.B w
is the only thing that changes the file.
.SS Other options
.B -q
starts silent.
.B -t
runs the self test and needs no window.
.B -s
plays nine hundred frames and writes the last one to
.B camber.raw
as 24-bit BGR at the window's scale;
.B -n
gives a different number of frames, which is how to look at a particular
part of a course.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/camber.art
the sheets, the font and the palettes, as tiles(2) commands
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/camber.chr
the 8x8 track tiles
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/camber16.chr
the machines, sixteen pixels square
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/ridge.trk
the course raced by default
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/camber.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tiles (2),
.IR sh-tiles (1),
.IR sprocket (1),
.IR strider (1),
.IR scorestore (2)
.PP
.B /doc/games-plan.md
says why this game is in the tree and what the others demonstrate.
.SH BUGS
.B -t
drives the rules and the design mode's editing directly.
It does not press any key: the tables that turn a key into an action,
and the repeat in the designer's cursor, are covered by nothing and have
only ever been checked by playing.
.PP
Nothing checks that a course is finishable.
A design mode that can write a wall of mud across every lane can write a
course that cannot be completed, and
.B -e
will let it.
.PP
A course may declare fewer than four lanes and the parser will take it,
but the bands are laid out for four: a two-lane course paints two of
them and leaves the rest of the screen as verge.
No course here uses fewer, so nothing has ever drawn one.
.PP
Rivals are held up by mud and pushed along by strips, but they do not
use ramps: they have no attitude to fly and simply ride through.
.PP
The machine is drawn in four attitudes rather than rotated, so the
picture in the air steps between them instead of turning.
